  "_style": "Address the user with the singular gị/i/ị (warm, informal; never the formal or plural for one person) and the couple with unu / unu abụọ (“you two”), and “anyị” when the couple speaks of themselves (“Ubi anyị”, “Osisi anyị”). The other person is “onye gị” (“your person”); Igbo pronouns ya/o/ọ/ha are already gender-neutral, so never add nwoke/nwaanyị, di/nwunye, nne/nna or any gendered word, and do not use “di na nwunye” for the couple. Write standard Igbo orthography with the dotted vowels ị ọ ụ and ṅ (precomposed Unicode), no tone marks except where needed to disambiguate (e.g. ìhè “light”); keep loanwords in Igbo spelling (foto, akaụntụ, profaịlụ, wijetị) and keep Coupley, Premium, CoupleSync and AI as is. Quotes are curly “ ” outside and ‘ ’ inside, the ellipsis is one character …, no space before ? ! : ;, keep “ · ”, \\n, emoji and every placeholder exactly. Numbers use Western digits with a comma for thousands and a period for decimals (1,250 · 4.5); dates read day before month (14 Nọvemba 2026) or follow the system format. Buttons and actions are short imperatives (Chekwaa, Zipu, Nwaa ọzọ, Kụọ osisi) without “biko”; titles, tabs, chips and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first word only) except proper names. Igbo nouns do not inflect for number, so the only plural category is [\"other\"]: write one form that reads naturally with any count (“%lld ụbọchị”). A partner's name placeholder %@ is never changed, prefixed or fused with another word (no possessive or case attached to it); rephrase the sentence instead (“%@ zitere gị akwụkwọ ozi” rather than reshaping the name). Tone: warm, light, kind, natural Igbo, never cheesy or word-for-word.",
